===Glyph origin===
{{Han etyl|人}}
{{liushu|p}} – side view of a standing man, highlighting the arms and legs. Compare {{l-lite|zh|sc=Hani|大}}.

===Etymology 1===
{{zh-forms|alt=亻-component,𠆢-component,⺈-component,儿-component,𠂉-component,几,𠔽,𤯔,𦉫,寧-Northern Wu}}
{{zh-ref|Sagart (1999)}} relates it to {{cog-lite|bo|sc=Tibt|ཉེན||relative|tr=nyen}}, from {{m-lite|bo|sc=Tibt|ཉེ||near|tr=nye}} + nominalizing suffix ''-n''; if so, from {{inh-lite|zh|sit-pro|*s-naːj ~ s-nej||near}}.

{{zh-ref|Schuessler (2007)}} proposes two etymologies:
* Same etymon as {{och-l|仁|to be kind; to be good}}, from {{inh|zh|sit-pro|*s/k-niŋ||heart; brain; mind}}. The semantic association of {{lang-lite|zh|sc=Hani|仁}} with {{lang-lite|zh|sc=Hani|人}} was somewhat late, dated to [[Mencius]]'s time; later on, {{lang-lite|zh|sc=Hani|仁}} would be usually interpreted as "humane, acting like a human being".
** If so, semantically parallel with {{ncog-lite|bo|sc=Tibt|སེམས་ཅན||living being, human, animal|lit=possessor of mind|tr=sems can}}; and {{noncog-lite|la|animalis||living, having soul}} from {{m-lite|la|anima||soul}}.
* Related to {{cog-lite|mkh-pro|*ɲaʔ ~ *ɲah||person}} with a nominalizing suffix ''-n'', from the auto-ethnonym of a hostile, possibly {{w|Austroasiatic languages|Austroasiatic-speaking}}, {{w|Yueshi culture|eastern}} people, whom the Sinitic-speaking {{w|Shang dynasty|Shang}} referred to as {{och-l|人方|id=1,1}}.

